---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import FlowDemo from '../../components/FlowDemo.astro';
import CTA from '../../components/CTA.astro';
---

<Layout>
  <section class="product-hero flow-hero">
    <div class="container product-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="product-label">
          <span class="app-icon blue" aria-hidden="true">
            ↗
          </span>{' '}
          CIVOLYN Flow
        </p>
        <h1>
          Let the busywork
          <br />
          <em class="text-blue">move itself.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="intro-copy">
          Some work needs your best thinking. Some work just needs a clear next
          step. Flow imagines a better way to tell the difference.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="button button-blue" href="#preview">
            Try the local preview <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
          </a>
          <a class="text-button" href="/compare/">
            Compare products ↗
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="disclosure">
          <span class="status-dot"></span> Fictional product · No real
          automation or integrations
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-preview-art" id="preview">
        <FlowDemo />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="note-strip">
    <div class="container">
      <span>
        Every action in this preview happens locally. No brief is received and
        no project is created.
      </span>
      <a href="/support/#privacy">About the examples ↗</a>
    </div>
  </div>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container product-benefits">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">STRUCTURE WHERE IT HELPS</p>
        <h2>
          Make the routine clear.
          <br />
          <em>Keep judgment human.</em>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li>
          <span class="number">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Start with a visible trigger.</h3>
            <p>
              A client brief, an internal request or a reporting date could
              begin a process. Define the starting point so people know why the
              work is happening.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Give each step a purpose.</h3>
            <p>
              Collect the relevant context, route a task and identify the next
              owner. The conceptual builder favours understandable steps over a
              tangle of hidden rules.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Pause for a person.</h3>
            <p>
              Before a proposal leaves the team, let someone read it. The
              preview stops at a review checkpoint and only continues when you
              approve the example.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Leave a readable trail.</h3>
            <p>
              Status changes show what is ready, waiting or complete. A useful
              workflow history would explain the handover, rather than merely
              record a timestamp.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section feature-demo-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">FAMILIAR WORK. A MORE CONSIDERED PATH.</p>
      <h2>
        Think of the tasks
        <br />
        <em>that keep coming back.</em>
      </h2>
      <div class="use-case-row">
        <article>
          <h3>A warmer onboarding.</h3>
          <p>
            Move from an approved brief to a first milestone with less chasing.
            Make the handover clear before the team begins.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>A calmer approval queue.</h3>
          <p>
            Know what needs a decision, which context to read and who is
            responsible for the next move.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>A useful weekly report.</h3>
          <p>
            Bring prepared updates together, ask for a review and make the final
            summary easier to understand.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="boundary-note">
        <p>
          <b>A product idea, not a connected service.</b> Flow’s preview is a
          deterministic four-step example. There is no workflow editor,
          background execution, message delivery or external connection. Refresh
          to return to the beginning.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split-section">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">BETTER WITH CONTEXT</p>
        <h2>
          A process is only as useful
          <br />
          <em>as what it understands.</em>
        </h2>
        <p>
          Knowledge keeps the example playbook close. Signal makes the pattern
          of work easier to explore. Together, the three concepts tell a more
          complete story.
        </p>
        <a class="arrow-link" href="/platform/">
          See how the platform connects <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="design-demo">
        <p class="eyebrow">A QUESTION BEFORE AUTOMATION</p>
        <h3>Which decision should stay with a person?</h3>
        <p>
          Start there. Then work backwards to the information, ownership and
          steps that make that decision easier.
        </p>
        <div class="preview-description">
          In this example: a person approves the proposed project handover
          before it becomes ready for the team.
        </div>
        <a class="arrow-link" href="/journal/#responsible-automation">
          Read about responsible automation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CTA />
  <div class="container product-nav">
    <a href="/platform/">← Back to the platform</a>
    <a href="/products/knowledge/">Next: Knowledge ↗</a>
  </div>
</Layout>
